• The plan is ready for a spike. The latest comments supersede the earlier
  proposals: no workmux integration, explicit remote repository paths,
  unconditional captures, and a configured dedicated tmux server. Those simplify
  the design. Updated plan (https://github.com/laat/laatmux/issues/1)

  Three points remain:

  1. Milestone one contradicts the session-ownership rule. Agents are started
     manually in tmux panes, but laatmux only attaches to sessions it created.
     Specify a minimal session launcher or an explicit convention for manually
     creating eligible sessions on the laatmux server. Otherwise the jump
     acceptance test has no defined setup.

  2. Hook invalidation still needs precise semantics. What makes a screen signal
     “newer”—a new observation or an actual screen transition? A freshly captured
     screen can contain an old prompt. Also, only idle/blocked signals invalidate
     hooks: an old done report could mask subsequent visible working activity if
     the next hook is missed. I’d omit hook influence from milestone one and prove
     screen detection first; later define reconciliation for all activity states.
     Hook decision
     (https://github.com/laat/laatmux/issues/1#issuecomment-5764239829)

  3. Sandbox process identification is a hypothesis to test. Reading the
     foreground group leader’s arguments doesn’t establish that the leader is
     Claude; it might still be a wrapper. The spike should explicitly test Claude
     exiting and restarting beneath a surviving wrapper, plus foreground changes
     during tool execution. These must distinguish an agent restart from a
     temporary foreground change. Identity specification
     (https://github.com/laat/laatmux/issues/1)

  The path-based repository decision looks settled. Keep origin-based grouping
  purely presentational so two clones remain independently selectable. Latest
  comment (https://github.com/laat/laatmux/issues/1#issuecomment-5764278901)

  I wouldn’t expand the design review further before implementation. Resolve the
  manual-session setup, bound the hook behavior, and let the spike answer the
  process and terminal questions.

